  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000119990,
      "SummaryText": "Got Propane, Inc. has developed a Risk Management Program to address the United States Environmental Protection Agency\u0092s Risk Management Plan (US EPA RMP) requirements (Code of Federal Regulations, Title 40, Part 68 [40 CFR \u00a768]). The facility is extremely diligent in the handling of all chemicals and dedicated to the safety of its employees and the neighboring community. The facility\u0092s staff is trained and utilizes modern equipment to monitor the facility and provide safeguards.\n\nGot Propane, Inc. \u0096 Los Angeles Storage Facility is located at 638 E Gage Ave, Los Angeles, CA 90001. The regulated substance at the facility is propane. The maximum intended inventory of propane is 179,576 gallons (761,400 pounds [lbs.]) and it is stored in a pressurized horizontal storage tank.\n\nThe covered process has over the 10,000-lb threshold quantity for US EPA RMP. As a result, the covered process is subject to US EPA RMP Program Level 3. The Propane Prevention Program 3 elements include:\n\n\u0095\tProcess Safety Information\n\u0095\tProcess Hazard Analysis\n\u0095\tOperating Procedures\n\u0095\tTraining\n\u0095\tMechanical Integrity\n\u0095\tManagement of Change\n\u0095\tPre-Startup Safety Review\n\u0095\tCompliance Audits\n\u0095\tIncident Investigation\n\u0095\tEmployee Participation\n\u0095\tHot Work Permit\n\u0095\tContractors\n\nGot Propane, Inc. \u0096 Los Angeles Storage Facility is a non-responding facility for all releases of propane. The facility has implemented an EAP that meets applicable requirements for notifying local first responders. Employees are trained to extent needed for their responsibilities. Got Propane, Inc. coordinates with local Fire Department and other first responders. \n\nCurrently, the only planned changes to improve safety come from the recommendation founds in the 2024 Process Hazard Analysis Report. There have been no accidental releases at the facility within the last five years."
    }
